; NEGATIVE_CACHE_LIKELY.
;
; RFC 2308: the negative-cache TTL is min(SOA MINIMUM, the SOA RR's own TTL).
; Here MINIMUM is 3600 but the SOA TTL is 300, so the correct answer is 300.
; Reading MINIMUM alone is the classic bug, and it makes us tell a customer to
; wait an hour when they could retry in five minutes.
;
; What is testable here is the *computed* TTL from the authority-section SOA of
; an NXDOMAIN, asserted directly against dns-auth with no caching in the path.
; What is not testable is actual hour-long expiry — the repo bans sleeps, so the
; clock is injectable and the expiry arithmetic is unit-tested separately.
$ORIGIN negcache-low.test.
$TTL 300

@       IN SOA  ns1.test. hostmaster.propgate.invalid. (
                1          ; serial
                7200       ; refresh
                3600       ; retry
                1209600    ; expire
                3600 )     ; minimum — deliberately larger than the SOA's own TTL
@       IN NS   ns1.test.

exists  IN A    198.51.100.70
; Everything else under this zone is NXDOMAIN, which is the point.
